    "go-goroutines-learn": {
      "id": "go-goroutines-learn",
      "title": "Learn: start one, pass a value",
      "objectives": [
        "Explain what the go keyword starts and why the caller keeps running alongside it.",
        "Trace an unbuffered send and receive and say which side waits until the other is ready.",
        "Implement FanSum with n goroutines and exactly n receives, and explain why the count is the wait."
      ],
      "kind": "lesson",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "go-goroutines"
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "A goroutine is any function you start with the go keyword: go work(). It runs on its own, concurrently with the code that started it. The safe way for goroutines to cooperate is a channel — a typed pipe you send values into and receive them out of — so the sharing is done by passing values, not by both sides poking one variable."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "Start a goroutine, send a value, receive it:",
          "code": "ch := make(chan int)\n\ngo func() { ch <- 42 }()\n\nn := <-ch   // blocks until the goroutine sends, so n is 42"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "make(chan int) opens an unbuffered channel: a send waits until a receiver is ready, and the other way round. That handoff is the synchronisation — once FanSum has received n values it knows all n goroutines have sent, so it can stop counting. No sleep, no guessing."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "go f() starts f on its own goroutine; the caller keeps running.",
            "A channel passes values; an unbuffered one blocks until its partner is ready.",
            "Count the receives; reading exactly what you expect means the work is done."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "In chan.go, make FanSum start n goroutines that each send their index on a channel, receive all n values, and return their sum. If go is missing from your PATH, run the environment check first."
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "FanSum must launch n goroutines with the go keyword, not add the numbers in the loop that starts them.",
        "A send on an unbuffered channel blocks until someone receives, so a loop that reads n values needs no WaitGroup."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Adding to a shared total from inside each goroutine instead of sending the value down the channel — that is sharing memory, not communicating.",
        "Believing a goroutine sends the value you meant — it reads the variable when it runs, so anything the loop changes after that is what arrives. Passing the index in as an argument pins it."
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "goroutine-timeline",
        "spec": {
          "lanes": [
            "main",
            "worker"
          ],
          "events": [
            {
              "lane": "main",
              "step": 0,
              "label": "go work(ch)"
            },
            {
              "lane": "worker",
              "step": 1,
              "label": "ch <- 42"
            },
            {
              "lane": "main",
              "step": 1,
              "label": "n := <-ch"
            },
            {
              "lane": "main",
              "step": 2,
              "label": "FanSum stops"
            }
          ],
          "links": [
            {
              "from": 1,
              "to": 2,
              "label": "42"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "Effective Go: goroutines",
          "url": "https://go.dev/doc/effective_go#goroutines"
        }
      ]
    },
    "go-goroutines-ask": {
      "id": "go-goroutines-ask",
      "title": "Reason: why the handoff is safe",
      "kind": "question",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "go-goroutines"
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "FanSum starts 5 goroutines and then reads 5 values off an unbuffered channel, summing them. It uses no WaitGroup and no sleep. Why is it still guaranteed to see all five sends?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        "The goroutines finish so fast that they are always done before the loop reads, so timing hides it.",
        "Go schedules every goroutine to completion before main runs at all, so order cannot vary.",
        "An unbuffered channel stores all five values the instant the goroutines start, so nothing is lost.",
        "A send only completes when a receive takes the value, so five receives prove five sends."
      ],
      "answer": 3,
      "explanation": "An unbuffered channel is a rendezvous: a send blocks until a receiver is ready to take that exact value, and the sender only proceeds after the handoff. Five receives therefore cannot all complete unless five sends handed over a value, so reading the expected count is itself the wait — no WaitGroup or timer needed. A buffered channel is different: sends could finish into the buffer before anyone read, so a completed send would no longer prove a receive happened.",
      "hints": [
        "Ask what a send waits for on an unbuffered channel — the answer is a receiver."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Adding to a shared total from inside each goroutine instead of sending the value down the channel — that is sharing memory, not communicating.",
        "Believing a goroutine sends the value you meant — it reads the variable when it runs, so anything the loop changes after that is what arrives."
      ]
    },
